A||N||4||TwisT||Need Advice:  How to prepare a Fiero for paint! ||||12||
Z||000000||TwisT||09-24-2000||07:22 PM||||Hey gang...  my '88 GT is now all stripped down of it's body panels and is ready for sanding and prepping.  I have all winter to get it ready for it's new Viper-Red paint.  Car is currently white.<P>I'm looking for some basic advice...  nothing is trivial here,  as I've never done this before.  <P>Can anyone help???  Thanks!<P> ||||reg||12
Z||000001||2xsess||09-24-2000||08:07 PM||||Ok, First you go to your local autobody supply store or someplace similar. You purchase a prep-solvent to remove any wax,grease and dirt. Do that before you even do anything else. This will help prevent a defect know as fisheye. I also recommend that you buy a sanding block or pad, it will help to make sure you sand evenly. If there are any nicks in the old paint you have to "feather" them out, that means, blend them in, I hope you understand what I mean by that. If there are any deep nicks you can use a finishing putty to help fill them in. Don't sand so hard that you cause dimples! If you have an air compressor, a Dual Action Sander will be extremely helpful. Are you doing the painting too? If not, ask the painter what grit he prefers you to sand the panels with as different primers have different fill properties. Then sand and sand and sand. <P>Hope that helps you get started. As I said in the begining, makes sure you use the prep solvent before you even think of sanding because you can possibly sand any oils or wax deeper into the old paint.<P>Good Luck!!!<P><BR>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT<p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 09-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||09-24-2000||08:10 PM||||Well, I've just been there.  Not much fun, but the end results are great!  Well to start, you might want to wet-sand all the way down to the fiberglass.  I didn't do this, but it might have been better if I had.  I used an 800-1000 grit on the WHOLE thing down to the primer.  That took about 2 months, every day, during my summer break.  You might want to use a heavier grit paper, something like 450-600?  I also didn't have the panels removed, so it will be easier for you.  Will be a really nice job too, if the preparation is good.  You will then want to use filler on the chips and deep scratches, if any.  Sand all down smooth.  This is an important step.  The paint guy didn't sand two of the fills down, and now you can see the outlines of them in the paint.  Then you want to primer it and crap, paint, wetsand after clearcoated to get all the bugs and dust out, and then buff with a glazing compound.  Turns out really nice.  I am going to wet-sand and buff it again next summer to get all the scratches, and probably key marks, out.  Stupid jealous high school kids.  This probably didn't help you too much, but that's about the basics of everything.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||09-24-2000||08:31 PM||||If the original paint is good, and it has not been painted several times before, there is no need to take the original finish all the way off. On areas that will need to be sanded and primed, you can use 180-240 dry paper. If the finish is still in good shape, wet sand it with 400 until it is smooth and all gloss has been removed. Usually sealer and paint has a hard time sticking to something sanded with paper above 600 grit unless you use a mid coat adhesion promoter. Probably the most important thing to remember is to make sure you sand around the edges very well. Because you have the panels off, this should be a piece of cake.||||reg||1
Z||000004||TwisT||09-24-2000||08:47 PM||||Thanks gang,  keep the input coming!!!<P>Cooter,  the car has in fact been painted once before.  Original color was white,  and the second coat of paint was "ok" at best.  I won't be painting the car,  just prepping.  Goal will be to have an excellent finish without spending tons of dough.  (of course!)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
